 "requested_at": "2026-11-11T15:58:30.904883Z",
 "text": "The clause is enforceable. Section 14(1) of the *Residential Tenancies Act, 2006* voids any tenancy agreement provision that contravenes the Act, is inconsistent with it, or waives a tenant's rights under it. This clause does none of those things. It restates a tenant's existing statutory duty not to cause damage to the unit, and the Act's rules on disturbance and reasonable enjoyment apply to animals as they do to any other source of disruption. A landlord can rely on it as a tenant obligation, and a tenant cannot avoid it on the ground that the Act voids it.\n\nThe clause does not ban animals, so it does not raise the separate question of whether a no-pets term is enforceable. Nor does it purport to override any right under the Act. Section 14(1) is the section that decides enforceability, but I have not checked the current consolidated text, so confirm the wording and any amendments on e-Laws before relying on this.\n\nVerdict: enforceable\nSection: 14",
